in november , the police arrested nada zatouna , an independent filmmaker who was filming clashes between protesters and the police in mohamed mahmoud st . , near tahrir square .
nada told that she was treated harshly and beaten by the police . " i was shocked to find a rubber bullet in my left leg .
policemen beat me and used electric sticks on me and seized my camera , " said nada .
" not the right time . "
last january , during the egyptian uprising that ousted president hosny mubark , egyptian women were in the frontline alongside men and suffered from the tear gas and rubber bullet .
nevertheless , shortly after mubark was ousted , women started to feel that their rights were ignored in post-revolutionary egypt .
only three weeks after mubarak was ousted , hundreds of women were shocked when they staged a demonstration in tahrir square to celebrate international women 's day in an attempt to draw attention to egyptian women 's issues .
they found themselves being shouted down by groups of men who told them that it was not the right time to be making a fuss about women 's issues .
it was a disappointing moment . women who faced tear gas and bullets were told by some guys that they had better stay at home .
among those women was sally zohney , a 24 -year-old twitter activist who works for the group un women .
writings about the revolution
on her twitter account , sally describes herself as bold , with a progressive feminist ideology .
sally was one of the volunteers on the barricades put up around tahrir square last january , checking the identities and handbags of people coming in .
sally was extremely disappointed by the reaction against the women 's demonstration , especially the claim that it was not the right time for women 's issues .
she says that the concept of " not the right time " doesn 't make sense . women have been there taking part from the very beginning of the revolution , " she said .
the day after international women 's day , the military police decided to clear the square of the sit-in , which was calling for the resignation of ahmed sahfik 's government . they arrested hundreds of people , amongst whom were 17 women , who were taken first to the egyptian museum where they were interrogated .
samira ibrahim , 25 , was among them . she had made the journey from sohag to take part in the revolution .
" i had always dreamt of visiting the egyptian museum , but it never occurred to me that when i would finally make that visit , it would be to be beaten and to receive electric shock , " she said .
samira and the other women were then taken to a military prison where the warden told them to stand in two lines , one for married women and the other for unmarried ones .
samira stood in the latter .
" in the room there was a woman who told me to take off my clothes , because they wanted to check if i was a virgin or not .
women at tahrir
i told her this was illegal and they had no right to ask me to strip naked in front of everyone . but after they had given me another electric shock , i stopped resisting .
the test was done by a man who used his hand . it took him five minutes to do it , " samira said .
samira brought a court action against the scaf to stop similar virginity tests from happening again .
in december , cairo 's administrative court ruled that the tests were illegal .
at first the scaf denied that the tests had taken place , but later a scaf general admitted on a tv show that they had taken place . " those girls are not like your daughter or mine , " he said .
" these were girls who had camped out in tents with male protesters in tahrir square . "
samira had told the bbc that she was boycotting the current parliamentary election . she said that she couldn 't trust the election to be fair and free as long as the scaf was in charge .
but on the first day of voting in november , huge numbers of women turned out to cast their vote .
they stood in line and waited their turn . although they had to wait for a long time to get in and cast their votes , up to several hours , they waited with a smile on their faces .
in front of a polling center in the nasr city district in eastern cairo , one of them told me : " it 's the first time i have voted in an election where i didn 't know the result beforehand . "
egyptian women played a significant role in the revolution that ousted the old regime .
one of the women in the line was a 19 -year-old , sarah mohamed . she wore a blue headscarf and a long dress . she told me that she is a member of the muslim brotherhood ( mb ) .
sarah said that her dream was that women would play a bigger role in egyptian political life .
women 's representation
the muslim brotherhood 's freedom and justice party ( fjp ) is the biggest party in the new parliament .
it is interesting to see substantial female support for a movement whose organizational structure , the brotherhood consultative council and guidance bureau , does not include any women .
furthermore , women are poorly represented in the new parliament .
women were able to secure only a mere 3 % of the post-revolution parliament .
sarah said she was disappointed by that . the dominant culture must change and people have to be aware that women have a bigger role to play in political life . "
sarah attributed the poor representation of women to [ the fact that ] women were placed so low on party lists .
" party lists do include female candidates but they are at the bottom of the lists , so they have little chance of being elected . "
although they were in the front lines alongside men ,
sarah is not alone in her disappointment about women [ not ] being represented in parliament .
sally zohney , in another tweet , wrote " it 's a pity that all the women in these lines are not represented in parliament . "
in 2010 , a decision was issued to reserve sixty-four seats in parliament for women , in what came to be termed the " women 's quota " . after mubarak was ousted , this quota was revoked .
sally zohney says that although that quota secured numerical representation for women , the appointed women were not representative of egyptian woman . they were isolated from the needs of egyptians on the streets .
she believes that despite the current poor representation of women in parliament , society will come to realize the importance of having women in parliament .
according to sally , " for the time being , it is important to emphasize quality , not quantity . "
determination
despite the disappointment of the women who took part in the revolution , they are determined to claim their rights .
in december , thousands of women marched in solidarity with the woman who had her clothes torn open and was beaten by the military police .
the women raised signs that read " egyptian women are a redline " . this prompted the scaf to post a statement on its facebook page that read : " the supreme council of the armed forces extends its deep apologies to the great women of egypt for the violations that took place amid the recent events in front of the people 's assembly and the cabinet . "
a year on , egyptian women feel that they are being marginalized .
nada still goes to tahrir .
" they think they can break us by torture ,
but now we have grown stronger and more determined , " she said .
sally still tweets about women 's rights , and says " we have to fight for our rights ; rights are not granted , rights are earned . the 25 th of january taught us that . "
last december , the administrative court revoked the military decision to subject female demonstrators to compulsory virginity tests .
when she learned about the decision , samira felt guardedly happy .
very soon , however , she came to realize that her confrontation with the scaf was not over yet .
in december , a photo was published showing army soldiers beating a girl so that her clothes were torn open , while the soldiers were disbursing the protesters calling on the supreme council of the armed forces ( scaf ) to step down and hand over power to a civilian presidential council .
the image sparked widespread criticism in egypt and abroad for the actions of scaf and its ability to assume the leadership of the country in the transitional period .
local human rights groups accused the scaf of targeting women in particular , in an attempt to deter them from taking part in demonstrations .
this wasn 't the first time women had been violently mistreated by the police .
