i really felt that the bloc wanted to make a show and the muslim brotherhood wanted to control everything ,
while the salafists were unexpectedly quiet and acted with great professionalism .
but i hope they won 't be like that with the muslim brotherhood because there should be a strong opposition in parliament for the benefit of egypt , because it 's not in egypt 's interest to have one party ruling it .
may allah grant all of them success !
maybe i was wrong and some people were angry with me . i respect their points of view , and the matter will continue to be relative and open to differences of opinion .
second : the conflict of electing the parliament speaker .
all of us got a negative impression , after the speaker 's election skirmishes , but the harshness and nervousness which prevailed were healthy aspects , in my opinion .
i think that the muslim brotherhood members were mistaken when they insisted on preventing essam sultan from introducing himself to the mps in accordance with the regulations .
a number of those members stood up and shouted , " sit down , sit down , " which made me very angry .
in the end , the president allowed all mps to introduce themselves to those who wanted to vote for them .
there was an important message which i conveyed to the muslim brotherhood on that day , after those events , namely , that their party had a relative majority so they shouldn 't just try to show their voting power and provoke conflicts with other mps . rather , they should be patient in dealing with the rest of the mps .
politically , i think that their way of dealing with essam sultan was not good .
this may be because of essam sultan 's personality and the history of disagreement between essam sultan and the muslim brotherhood .
and i think that if it were someone else , the situation would have turned out differently .
the first day of parliament
anyway , this stage ended and the session 's atmosphere became positive after the completion of the deputies ' election ,
and i hope that we will quickly move beyond this bickering .
third : i refused to send a telegram to the scaf , and i clearly said that if the majority supported sending it , it would have to be amended to make the text balanced and must record my official rejection .
and i think that this telegram showed a positive reaction from the muslim brotherhood , as a positive response to the field marshal 's telegram , which contained a number of positive points , but they couldn 't write it in an appropriate way .
fourth : most of the speeches confirmed that the revolution would continue , and that the first priority would be to regain the rights of the martyrs and the people who were injured .
this was a positive matter which boded well , so tomorrow 's session would be devoted to discussing the file of the martyrs and the rights of the people who were injured , as well as retaliation .
that should be counted as one of the positive aspects of the parliament 's work .
fifth : at the humanitarian level , a spirit of affection and kindness prevailed among most members .
almost all members shook hands and embraced one another .
i still think that the al-nour party 's mps were very humble and well-mannered , which would make you apprehensive to argue with them , even if you have a different opinion .
i do not think i need to recount the events of the first day of parliament , which were watched by millions via satellite channels , and during which the positions of all the mps were observed .
i think the atmosphere will gradually improve a lot , and we will move away from an atmosphere of claiming to be more nationalistic than others and starting conflicts , and will focus on content .
finally , the whole scene is positive , and i will continue to fulfill my message in search of points of consensus and unity , no matter what happens .
the last thing i want to say is that today i fel that parliament was the first and most important step to ending military rule and the handover of power .
support all the mps who will reveal the truth , so that they may continue to be the voice of conscience , which will guarantee the success of the revolution .
but i 'd like to focus on some brief points : the oath-taking issue .
i did not intend to violate the official oath out of respect for the law , the constitution and the traditions of the institution , which i have come to belong to .
what was done by mp mamdouh ismail , who added the phrase " in what is not against islamic law , " made the president of the session , mahmoud sakka , ask him to say the oath again without adding this phrase . however , he repeated the same oath and many mps did the same , which made me feel that this offended all mps because of the failure of the president to take any action against the member who challenged everyone .
therefore , i began my oath by saying that if the rule was broken once , we shouldn 't blame those who break it after that .
so i said the official oath and added , " i swear to work to complete the revolution and to regain the martyr 's rights . "
the assembly 's administration turned off the sound at that time .
in my opinion , i didn 't break the law and the rules , because the president 's lack of action with respect to breaking the rules allowed all members to add their own words .
i 'm really disappointed in parliament , and i wish they hadn 't broadcasted the session . it was awful .
i wished to see our parliament representing egypt in a good manner , but unfortunately some mps were waving " goodbye " at the camera ,
