i have one clear definite proposal for enforcing the law and prohibiting sit-ins , whether for or against the current crisis .
any violator is to be arrested on the spot with no regard to names , affiliations , or offices , provided that violence is not used at all .
the arrested can be jailed until all phases of the schedule are implemented and the pillars of the state are deeply rooted .
i had watched a recorded episode of wael el-abrashy 's show and another one that followed of el-meslamany 's program .
human rights groups can be allowed to visit the arrested at any time so that no torture or violation of their rights takes place . that is all ok , even if the groups want to be with them in the detention centers .
my goal is to not give excuses to those who are pursuing power through any unlawful means and even the destruction of the country .
this way , we can guarantee that we won 't have any more casualties , thugs , scoundrels , or any of that stuff that we are fed up with ; it serves no good .
at least , we can bring the country into a more peaceful mode and preserve the remaining institutions . we have had enough fires and casualties .
we can also pay attention to the presidential elections and examine the candidates ' platforms , without having to make our choices under the pressure of demonstrations , sit-ins and similar chaos .
frankly speaking , if the scaf does not take strict measures shortly , i will start to suspect that they are happy with this chaos .
i 'm not convinced of the scaf 's strategy not to enforce the law .
this cannot be out of concern for the revolutionaries , martyrs ' rights or any similar stuff .
this is chaos , nothing more nor less .
el-meslamany started his episode by playing a video footage of thugs preventing fire engines from putting a fire out . they were fighting the driver and shouting at him " why do you want to put it out ? "
the driver was assaulted and finally they took the car and drove it away .
in his episode , el-abrashy interviewed the " esteemed " activist ahmed abou douma . he admitted that he had thrown molotov cocktails at the people 's assembly and any building where there were officers . he went on to say that he was targeting the " bad " officers , not the buildings .
oh little darling boy , would molotov cocktails attack the officers selectively without setting fire to the buildings , which were erected by public funds and will be repaired by public funds ?
this is an implicit admission that they recruit thugs to throw molotov cocktails and the like .
there was another guy called essam from the april 6 th movement . he insisted that they are not at fault and that they all have the right to bring the scaf down .
el-abrashy tried every way he could to convince him , but the guy had one single reply , that they would never break their sit-in unless the scaf left power .
el-abrashy tried once more , saying that the scaf announced a schedule that would take a few months , and then they would leave power , but this did not seem to make the boy feel any better .
i would rate the post as excellent . however , i do not agree with you about arresting all those who take to the streets . sit-ins and demonstrators are lawful rights in themselves . but when they lead to blocking the way and bringing normal life to a halt , they turn into chaos .
