no one asked me in america about my degree or about my wealth .
the only question that i was asked : " what do you do for a living ? "
when my daughter travelled this summer to europe to study , she worked for six continuous weeks during her summer vacation to pay me the price of the travel ticket .
she taught me another lesson when i saw her working at a starbucks store , serving customers , washing dishes , and cleaning the restaurant .
i was touched for a moment by what i considered to be humiliation for my daughter ( as per my old conception ) , but i immediately felt proud because she did what i could not do when i was her age , and i gave her a five dollar tip for a two-and-half dollar cup of coffee ! !
her male and female colleagues at the store were amazed and said to her :
in the name of allah the most merciful the most compassionate
that man left a five dollar tip for a cup of coffee -
therefore , my daughter replied saying :
don 't be astonished , he is my father !
i learned in america to respect manual labor and the manual worker no matter how simple he is .
i learned in america to serve myself by myself at home and at work .
i learned modesty in america ; if you attended a meeting at our company attended by the director of the company and the lowest employee in the company , you would not be able to recognize who is the director and who is the lowest employee , as each has a role in the meeting .
i learned in america that luck does not knock except on the doors of the hard-working .
i learned in america to be proud of what i do ( as long as i am skilled at it ) , and to like what i do if i cannot do what i like .
