the principle of reciprocity : defect or right ?
look , nassar !
returning a favor is not something that is wrong , because it 's in people 's nature .
for example , when i talk to you , and you respond in an indifferent manner ( no return ) , i will stop talking to you .
you 've hit the nail on the head , maisam , because many people , including myself , suffer because of this matter .
unfortunately , most people would only provide service when they think it would be reciprocated . you know , if we seek allah 's pleasure in all our acts , we won 't expect anything in return . i mean if one does a favor for allah 's sake , it will be completely different .
but the problem is , who among us seeks the pleasure of allah ?
please answer honestly
my greetings .
a question came to my mind , and i would like to know your answers .
as you all know , today we deal with each other based on the reciprocity principle . i mean , if you do something for me , you expect something in return , even if it 's just the word " thanks . "
do you think this is a flaw in our personalities ?
should you always give to others , without expecting anything from them ?
or has this become an indispensable requirment of the expectations of our time ?
is it a defect of our time or in us ?
or do you think that it is not a defect at all , and can be considered a right and claim ?
how do you deal with people ?
do you deal with them on the basis of this principle or another principle ?
my answer to your topic is very simple , maisam .
a person who expects something in return for his services should stop providing services because this is not a job ; it 's doing a good deed .
the proverb says , " do good deeds , but don 't expect anything in return . "
anyone who expects to get something in return should stop providing services , because he expects something in return , and in this case , it would not be considered giving .
when someone gives money to a poor or needy person , does he expect something in return ?
if someone saves someone from drowning , death etc . , does he expect something in return or continues to tell that person , " if i hadn 't saved you , you would be dead now . "
when you offer your neighbor help , whether in his field of work or in another area , do you expect any money or thanks ?
do you do favors so that people thank you or give you something in return ?
there is a difference between someone who thanks you willingly and one who is obligated to thank you .
there is one more thing , maisam , namely , mentioning your favors .
if we expect something in return for favors , we will be doing a job , not favors .
when an employee does his work , he 's not doing a favor because he expects something in return for it .
but if someone volunteers to work he will be doing good deeds because he doesn 't expect a return .
in principle , when you do a favor , you shouldn 't mention it .
some people like to show-off and be promient , which is a different matter .
my greetings to you .
you know , nassar , the humanitarian situations you stated leave no room for expecting anything in return .
what i mean is your relationship with your friends .
some friends may stop being friends with you if they discover that they won 't get any benefit or can just contact you when they need you .
does friendship work according to this principle ?
reason and logic say no .
some people deem it acceptable that both friends benefit from one another , as long as neither of them will be harmed .
this isn 't my opinion , of course .
unfortunately , the second type of people exist .
someone who does you a favor and keeps reminding you of it .
